In C++, for a class hierarchy like:
class Base1 { /* members */ };
class Base2 { /* members */ };
class Derived : public Base1, public Base2 { /* members */ };
The assignment Base2* pbase2 = new Derived; requires the compiler to generate code that adjusts the address of the new Derived object by the offset of the Base2 subobject. Implementation notes: The compiler uses static offsets known at compile time, often involving pointer arithmetic like pbase2 = static_cast<Base2*>((void*)derivedPtr + offset); where 'offset' is the size of preceding subobjects.
